{"results": [{"id": "hp_doc_9d99b32f06e229859614493a25e80a0434aec65e718d91cd36a380b503293b34", "title": "Marion, Alabama", "text": "Marion is a city in, and the county seat of, Perry County, Alabama, United States. As of the 2010 census, the population of the city is 3,686, up 4.8% over 2000. First known as Muckle Ridge, the city was renamed after a hero of the American Revolution, Francis Marion.", "score": 17.9515}, {"id": "hp_doc_6f6bf69dc742e460494b2e52a7e64b77419ae5a0dba2f707b00a5ddd1d3c9aa4", "title": "Francis Marion", "text": "Francis Marion (c. 1732 – February 27, 1795) was a military officer who served in the American Revolutionary War. Acting with the Continental Army and South Carolina militia commissions, he was a persistent adversary of the British in their occupation of South Carolina in 1780 and 1781, even after the Continental Army was driven out of the state in the Battle of Camden.", "score": 17.9255}, {"id": "hp_doc_2bb002e920b29ed09f4ad91979d92d2939eb255931674730be5982f2f1309f56", "title": "Marion Kennedy", "text": "Marion Kennedy or Marion Grace Kennedy (23 November, 1836 – 11 January, 1914) was a British classical scholar. She was a supporter of women's suffrage and higher education for women. She was born too early to take advantage of women's higher education and her father took the credit for some of her work. She helped to found and then worked for Newnham College where her ideas shaped its constitution.", "score": 12.8143}], "remaining_searches": 1}
